{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Additional live evidence of the drain's steady-state cost, plus a scope boundary.\n\nDuring /rebar-debug of the plan-review sign fence (2026-08-22), `rebar doctor` caught a detached enrich_drain child (pid 96630, spawned by the impl-2ddd-b session) holding the advisory enrich-drain lock 437s at 0.0% CPU before exiting -- i.e. blocked in a no-timeout network call (the enrich LLM) INSIDE the advisory lock, not computing. A successor child (pid 41121) then held it 131s+. So under sustained load the drain is near-continuously lock-held, consistent with this ticket's budget-overrun symptom.\n\nScope note for the dedicated-clone remedy (approved on the related drain ticket): isolating the drain in its own tracker clone is the right fix for the drain's OWN latency / long lock-hold here, but it does NOT fix the plan-review sign-fence bug (a83f-6348-a08c-401d). I measured the sign-fence HEAD churn as 100% foreground ticket writes with 0 drain digest commits across 21 collect() windows -- so the two problems have different churn sources and need separate fixes. Keep them decoupled."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787440018645315001,"uuid":"66dad192-0362-4586-bd6b-95651770fca9"}